## Goal
Show how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) relate to one another in gene-expression space: a principal component analysis of the gene-level counts, with samples coloured by `condition`, so that it is visible whether samples separate by condition and whether any sample is an outlier.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `rnaseq_bulk` / `01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |

Sample `condition` labels come from `00_data/rnaseq_bulk/samples.csv` (read-only): A1-A3 = `untreated`, B1-B3 = `treated`. The matrix has 200 genes x 6 samples (columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one per sample). The input path is re-resolved with `_system/resolve_artifact.py` at execution time, not hard-coded.

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio`. Prerequisite: numpy, pandas and matplotlib must already be present in it; nothing is installed. If any is missing the plan is blocked and I will report it rather than work around it.

1. Resolve the `counts_gene` artifact via `resolve_artifact.py`; read the matrix and `samples.csv`; check that the sample columns match the `sample_id`s exactly and that `condition` is present for all six.
2. Drop `gene_name`; keep `gene_id` as the index. Drop genes with zero counts in every sample (no genes are otherwise filtered). Counts are the nf-core length-scaled gene counts as delivered.
3. Transform: log2(count + 1). This is a simple variance-stabilising choice, not DESeq2's VST/rlog; with 6 samples it is adequate for a visual overview.
4. Select genes: use all genes remaining after step 2 (the matrix is only 200 genes, so no top-variance cut-off is applied).
5. PCA: centre each gene across samples (no scaling to unit variance), then SVD (`numpy.linalg.svd`) of the samples x genes matrix. Record the variance explained by every component.
6. Plot PC1 vs PC2 as a scatter, one point per sample, coloured by `condition` (two colours, with a legend), each point labelled with its sample ID, axis labels showing % variance explained. Save as PNG (300 dpi) and SVG.
7. Write a table of PC scores per sample (PC1..PC6, plus `condition`) and a table of variance explained per component.

No input file is modified; all writes go under `results/`. Scripts are saved under `scripts/` exactly as run.

